{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# `src/rebar/_commands/fsck.py` is at exactly 800/800 LOC\n\n## Context / Problem\n\n`src/rebar/_commands/fsck.py` is at **exactly 800** lines — the hard cap in\n`.github/module-size-limit.txt`, enforced by the CI module-size gate with no allowlist. Any\nchange to fsck of any size, including a one-line bug fix, cannot land today. This is the third\ninstance of the same pattern (`valiant-balladic-willet` did `passes.py`,\n`splurgy-witless-flamingo` did `_ref_lock.py`), so the remediation is framed as an\narchitectural rework of the fsck cluster rather than a mechanical split: the file is pinned\nbecause four concerns are fused in it, and each new check grows the fused whole.\n\nThe full concern map, the recurrence mechanism, the compatibility constraints, and the\nrationale for each cut are recorded as the architectural-analysis comment on this ticket\n(`defaceable-icy-antelope`). This description states the resulting plan.\n\n## Approach\n\nSeparate `fsck.py` by concern along call-graph seams that already exist. `fsck.py` today holds\nfour concerns; two of them already have a single internal call site, which is the seam a prior\nauthor cut and stopped at.\n\n1. **Enumeration + per-ticket validators** move to a new `src/rebar/_commands/fsck_scan.py`:\n   `_scan` (the store walk — JSON validity, CREATE presence, stale index.lock, snapshot\n   consistency, forward-compat event types, and the advisory ensures/authorship tallies) and\n   `_check_snapshot`, which `_scan` is the only caller of. This is the cluster that grows with\n   every check added.\n2. **Tracker-level health** moves to a new `src/rebar/_commands/fsck_tracker_health.py`:\n   `_tracker_health` and the four checks it fans out to — `_tracker_sync_status`,\n   `_branch_mismatch`, `_foreign_store_paths`, `foreign_store_path_list` — plus the\n   `_FSCK_GIT_TIMEOUT` watchdog they share. `_tracker_health` is already the single entry\n   point, called once from `_scan`; these checks share a shape the per-ticket checks do not\n   (each inspects the tracker as a whole via git/config, each yields at most one line, each\n   decides for itself whether that line is counted).\n3. **Report shaping + command driver** stay in `fsck.py`: `_transform_json` /\n   `_STRUCTURED_KINDS` plus `fsck_cli`, `_repair_cli`, `_run_live_repair`,\n   `_missing_tracker_result`. Report shaping is roughly 45 LOC on its own, so extracting it\n   would create a sub-100-LOC module, which the module-size policy forbids; it is also the\n   output half of the same command surface the driver owns. `fsck.py` therefore becomes what\n   its name should mean — the fsck **command**: parse argv, drive scan or repair, render.\n\nDependency direction is strictly one-way and matches the existing call graph: `fsck.py` ->\n`fsck_scan.py` -> {`fsck_tracker_health.py`, `fsck_repair.py`, `fsck_authorship.py`}. No new\nedges, no cycles.\n\nThe change is **behaviour-preserving**: identical findings, identical text and JSON output,\nidentical exit codes. No check logic, message string, or counted/informational classification\nis altered — code moves, and imports are rewired.\n\n`fsck.<symbol>` attribute access is load-bearing outside the module (`_store/gitutil.py` and\n`_commands/fsck_recover.py` import `_resolve_tracker_git_dir`; `_commands/tracker_maintenance.py`\nimports `foreign_store_path_list`; tests call `fsck._check_snapshot`, `fsck._scan`,\n`fsck._tracker_sync_status`, `fsck._foreign_store_paths`). Every one of those **calls** the\nsymbol; none monkeypatch it, so `fsck.py` keeps module-level re-exports for all of them and\nthose callers are left untouched. The two symbols that ARE monkeypatched on the `fsck` module\n(`_reconciler_in_flight`, `owned_repair_pause`) are consumed by `_run_live_repair`, which stays\nin `fsck.py`, so the trap flamingo recorded — a moved function resolving a patched dependency\nfrom the wrong module's globals — does not arise.\n\n`_engine_support/bridge_fsck.py` is out of scope: it is a different command (bridge audits with\nits own `main`) with no call edge to `_commands/fsck.py`. `fsck_repair.py` is out of scope: it\nis already a clean single-concern extraction.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/_commands/fsck.py` — becomes the command driver + report shaping; keeps\n  re-exports for external `fsck.<symbol>` callers.\n- `src/rebar/_commands/fsck_scan.py` — new; `_scan` + `_check_snapshot`.\n- `src/rebar/_commands/fsck_tracker_health.py` — new; `_tracker_health`, `_tracker_sync_status`,\n  `_branch_mismatch`, `_foreign_store_paths`, `foreign_store_path_list`, `_FSCK_GIT_TIMEOUT`.\n\nNo other file changes. Tests are not edited: their behavioural assertions and their\n`fsck.<symbol>` bindings must keep passing unchanged, which is the primary evidence that the\nrefactor preserved behaviour.\n\n## Testing\n\nNo new testable behaviour is introduced — this is a pure structural refactor, so the existing\nsuite is the oracle and is run unmodified:\n\n- The fsck suites (`tests/interfaces/store/test_fsck_*.py`,\n  `tests/interfaces/store/test_repair_pause_ownership_heldout.py`,\n  `tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_creation_channel_compaction.py`,\n  `tests/interfaces/store/test_fsck_snapshot_checks.py`) pass unchanged, covering both output\n  formats, the repair paths, and the direct `fsck._check_snapshot` / `fsck._scan` bindings.\n- `tests/unit/test_module_size_contract.py` passes (the module-size contract).\n- The full CI selection `pytest -m \"not integration and not external\"` passes, catching any\n  caller whose `fsck.<symbol>` binding the move broke.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] AC1: `wc -l src/rebar/_commands/fsck.py` reports under 800 with real margin (target at\n      or below 400 — verified by running the command).\n- [ ] AC2: every new module is at least 100 LOC and no `src/rebar` file exceeds 800 —\n      verified by `wc -l` on the two new files and by\n      `tests/unit/test_module_size_contract.py` passing.\n- [ ] AC3: `fsck.py` still resolves `_resolve_tracker_git_dir`, `foreign_store_path_list`,\n      `_check_snapshot`, `_scan`, `_tracker_sync_status` and `_foreign_store_paths` as module\n      attributes — verified by the unmodified tests and by `_store/gitutil.py`,\n      `_commands/fsck_recover.py` and `_commands/tracker_maintenance.py` being untouched.\n- [ ] AC4: the fsck test suite passes with its behavioural assertions unedited — verified by\n      `git diff --stat` showing no change under `tests/` and a green run of the fsck suites.\n- [ ] AC5: `make lint`, `make typecheck`, the ten `scripts/check_*.py` gates and the CI pytest\n      selection all pass with no new debt — verified by running each locally before pushing."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786599497272012001,"uuid":"6e38a26e-f628-4ba5-a9dd-1c887c60f019"}